  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why do airships use helium?",
      "a": "Helium is much lighter than air and, unlike hydrogen, it cannot burn. Early airships used hydrogen, which is even lighter, but fires like the Hindenburg disaster in 1937 showed how dangerous it was.",
      "asks": [
        "Why don't airships use hydrogen?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a ballonet?",
      "a": "A ballonet is an air bag inside the envelope. Pumping air in or letting it out keeps the envelope firm as the helium expands and shrinks with height, and front and back ballonets help tilt the nose up or down.",
      "asks": [
        "What are the air bags inside an airship?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does an airship steer?",
      "a": "Propellers push it along, and the tail fins have a rudder to turn left or right and elevators to point the nose up or down. Some modern airships have swivelling propellers for extra control near the ground.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you fly an airship?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between a blimp and a zeppelin?",
      "a": "A blimp has no rigid frame; its shape comes from the gas pressure inside. A zeppelin has a rigid skeleton inside the envelope. The modern Zeppelin NT is semi-rigid, with a partial frame.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a blimp a zeppelin?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why aren't airships used more today?",
      "a": "They are slow, easily pushed around by strong winds, and need huge hangars. Today they are used for advertising, sightseeing and research, and some companies are developing them to carry cargo.",
      "asks": [
        "Will airships come back?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Hindenburg, filled with flammable hydrogen, was destroyed by fire in 1937 in New Jersey.",
    "Helium is about seven times less dense than air at the same temperature and pressure."
